From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Joel Winarske <joel.winarske@linux.com>
Date: Wed, 18 Nov 2026 09:30:00 -0800
Subject: [PATCH] build/config: target sysroot for the target half only

target_sysroot applies to every toolchain in the list at the end of
sysroot.gni, the host one included. A cross build has two halves, and the
sysroot this layer passes is the OE recipe sysroot for one machine, so the
host half compiles an x86_64 translation unit against the target's headers
and glibc stops it:

  FAILED: clang_x64/obj/.../runtime/libdart_precompiler.version.o
  clang++ ... -m64 -march=x86-64 --target=x86_64-linux-gnu
    --sysroot=.../aarch64-oe-linux/dart-sdk/3.14.0-r0/recipe-sysroot
  recipe-sysroot/usr/include/bits/wordsize.h:23:2: error: "__WORDSIZE is
    not defined"

The bundled sysroots never hit this. They are selected inside a current_cpu
test, which gn re-evaluates per toolchain, so the host half gets x64's and
the target half gets the target's. One gn arg cannot do that, so the test
moves to where the arg is consumed. target_cpu is not re-evaluated, and
comparing the two is what separates the halves.

The host half keeps //buildtools/sysroot/linux, which is what the debian
branch above hands x64 and so what it was already building against. It is
not a detail that can be dropped: this half produces the cross gen_snapshot
that dart-sdk-tools stages and that runs on the build machine, and with no
sysroot at all it would fall through to the build container's own headers.

Scoped to the case where the sysroot arrived on the command line, which is
an empty dart_sysroot with a non-empty target_sysroot. An upstream build
takes neither new branch.

Upstream-Status: Inappropriate [oe-specific cross layout]
Signed-off-by: Joel Winarske <joel.winarske@linux.com>
---
diff --git a/build/config/sysroot.gni b/build/config/sysroot.gni
--- a/build/config/sysroot.gni
+++ b/build/config/sysroot.gni
@@ -50,14 +50,30 @@
   }
 }
 
+# An empty dart_sysroot with a non-empty target_sysroot means the sysroot came
+# in on the command line rather than from the block above. meta-flutter does
+# that, and what it passes is the OE recipe sysroot for one machine, so it
+# fits the target half of a cross build and nothing else. current_cpu is
+# re-evaluated per toolchain while target_cpu is not, which is what separates
+# the halves here. Without that test the host half compiles clang_x64/
+# objects with an x86_64 triple against the target sysroot, and glibc's own
+# headers stop it:
+#
+#   bits/wordsize.h:23:2: error: "__WORDSIZE is not defined"
+#
+# The host half keeps the sysroot it had before anything was passed, which is
+# also the one the cross gen_snapshot has always been built against.
 if ((current_toolchain == host_toolchain ||
      current_toolchain == default_toolchain ||
      current_toolchain == "${default_toolchain}_shared" ||
      current_toolchain == "${default_toolchain}_asan" ||
      current_toolchain == "${default_toolchain}_msan" ||
      current_toolchain == "${default_toolchain}_tsan") &&
-    target_sysroot != "") {
+    target_sysroot != "" && (dart_sysroot != "" || current_cpu == target_cpu)) {
   sysroot = target_sysroot
+} else if (is_linux && dart_sysroot == "" && target_sysroot != "" &&
+           current_cpu != target_cpu) {
+  sysroot = rebase_path("//buildtools/sysroot/linux", root_build_dir)
 } else if (is_android) {
   import("//build/config/android/config.gni")
   sysroot = rebase_path("$android_toolchain_root/sysroot", root_build_dir)
